"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][4]={"school_physics_en:M4-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M4-L1 – Distinguishing temperature, internal energy and heat<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Temperature, heat and particles<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A full cup of lukewarm water can contain more internal energy than a\nsingle droplet of hot water. <strong>Temperature<\/strong> is not the\nsame as total <strong>energy content<\/strong>. In thermal contact,\nenergy is transferred from the warmer to the cooler body. This process\nis called <strong>heating by energy transfer<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and physical model<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Temperature<\/strong> describes a body’s thermal state and\ndetermines the direction of spontaneous net heat transfer. It is often\nmeasured in degrees Celsius\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi><\/mi><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>);\nthe SI base unit of thermodynamic temperature is kelvin\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nThe conversion is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ϑ<\/mi><msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>273.15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T/\\mathrm{K}=\\vartheta/^\\circ\\mathrm{C}+273.15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA temperature <strong>difference<\/strong> of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the same size as a difference of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Internal energy<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincludes microscopic kinetic and interaction energies of the particles\nin a system. It depends on factors such as the amount of substance,\nphysical state and temperature. <strong>Heat<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis energy transferred across a system boundary because of a temperature\ndifference; heat is not a substance stored in a body. Energy can also\nenter through mechanical or electrical work.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original draft diagram:<\/strong> Two bodies at different\ntemperatures, an energy-transfer arrow, and separate labels for\ntemperature, internal energy and heat transfer.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_018_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two bodies labelled 60 °C and 20 °C, a transfer arrow, and three separate concept definitions.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two bodies labelled 60 °C and 20 °C, a\ntransfer arrow, and three separate concept definitions.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Temperature is a state quantity, internal energy belongs to the\nsystem, and heat is an energy transfer.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and application<\/h2>\n<p>One body is at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>60<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand another at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir temperature difference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta T=60-20=40\\,\\mathrm{K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir temperatures are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>333.15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">333.15\\,\\mathrm{K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>293.15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">293.15\\,\\mathrm{K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn thermal contact, net heat transfer is from the hotter to the cooler\nbody. We <strong>cannot calculate the transferred energy<\/strong> from\ntemperature readings alone: mass, material and conditions matter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe investigation<\/h2>\n<p>Using a suitable classroom thermometer and teacher supervision,\nmeasure the temperatures of two cups of water that have been kept in\ndifferent room-temperature locations. Then place them side by side\nwithout mixing. Do <strong>not<\/strong> use touch to judge the\ntemperatures; perception is not a calibrated measurement. Avoid hot\nwater. Record time and approximate instrument uncertainty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can two different masses of water have different internal\nenergies at the same temperature?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Convert\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>22<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">22\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto kelvin.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which is a process quantity: temperature, internal energy or\nheat?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two spheres are at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>42<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">42\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>17<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">17\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind their difference in kelvin.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Internal energy depends partly on amount or mass, not just on\ntemperature.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>22<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>273.15<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>295.15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=22+273.15=295.15\\,\\mathrm{K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Heat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nenergy transferred due to a temperature difference.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>42<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>17<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta T=42-17=25\\,\\mathrm{K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Ordinary language often speaks of “heat in a body”; physics\ndistinguishes stored internal energy from heat transfer. The simple\narrow represents the <strong>net<\/strong> direction, not actual particle\ntrajectories. Continue with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics curriculum: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n3.1, 3.4 and 3.11 · Thermal behaviour, heat transfer and energy · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.nist.gov/pml/special-publication-330/sp-330-section-2\">NIST\n– The International System of Units (SI), SP 330, Section 2<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nKelvin, Celsius temperature and temperature difference · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.bipm.org/en/web/guest/si-base-units/kelvin\">BIPM – SI\nBase Unit: Kelvin<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Definition of the SI temperature unit · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M4-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M4-L2 – Particle model and states of matter<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Temperature, heat and particles<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>An ice cube keeps its shape. Melted water takes the shape of a\ncontainer, while water vapour can spread throughout the available space.\nThese are three states of the same substance, not three different\nchemicals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and physical model<\/h2>\n<p>In the <strong>simple particle model<\/strong>, matter is represented\nby very small particles. In a <strong>solid<\/strong>, neighbours remain\nlargely fixed while particles vibrate around equilibrium positions. In a\n<strong>liquid<\/strong>, particles stay close together but can change\nneighbours. In a <strong>gas<\/strong>, they are typically much farther\napart and move freely between collisions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Melting<\/strong> changes a solid into a liquid;\n<strong>freezing<\/strong> reverses it. <strong>Vaporisation<\/strong>\nturns liquid into gas; <strong>condensation<\/strong> reverses it. Liquid\nwater can also <strong>evaporate<\/strong> below its boiling temperature.\nThese changes require or release energy; the temperature of the whole\nsample need not rise throughout a phase change.<\/p>\n<p>Each drawn dot is a <strong>model symbol<\/strong>. Particles do not\nbecome physically larger when a sample is gently heated; it is their\nspacing, motion and interactions that change.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original draft diagram:<\/strong> Three panels with equal\nnumbers of symbolic particles showing different arrangements and types\nof motion.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_019_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three panels with nine model particles each: regular solid arrangement, close but mobile liquid, and well-spaced gas.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three panels with nine model particles\neach: regular solid arrangement, close but mobile liquid, and\nwell-spaced gas.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The same particles may differ in mobility and separation across\nstates of matter.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and application<\/h2>\n<p>A closed system initially contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{g}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof water. If some liquid becomes vapour but no matter escapes, the\n<strong>total mass<\/strong> stays\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{g}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOnly its distribution between phases changes. <strong>Never heat a\ncompletely sealed glass vessel:<\/strong> dangerous pressure can\ndevelop.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe investigation<\/h2>\n<p>At room temperature, place a small drop of water on each of two\ncomparable surfaces so that the drops cover different areas. Predict\nwhich may dry faster and observe without extra heating. Note that air\nflow and humidity also affect the outcome. A drawing comparing ice,\nwater and vapour is an experiment-free alternative.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Match fixed neighbours, changing neighbours and large separations to\nthe three states.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can evaporation occur at room temperature?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Do water molecules grow in size when heated?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does melting ice change its chemical identity or particle\narrangement?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Solid: fixed neighbours; liquid: changing neighbours; gas: usually\nlarge separations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Some particles can escape the liquid even below its boiling\ntemperature.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; the model changes motion, separation and interactions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Particle arrangement and mobility change, but it remains water\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{H_2O}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Particle drawings are schematic and not to scale. Real materials have\ndifferent interactions and internal structures. The observation cannot\ndetermine an accurate latent heat. Continue with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics curriculum: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n3.1, 3.4 and 3.11 · Thermal behaviour, heat transfer and energy · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/waermelehre/innere-energie-waermekapazitaet/grundwissen/spezifische-schmelz-und-verdampfungswaerme\">LEIFIphysik\n– Specific latent heat of fusion and vaporisation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Energy transfer\nin phase changes and the particle model · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M4-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M4-L3 – Conduction, convection and thermal radiation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Temperature, heat and particles<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A metal spoon gradually warms along its handle in warm soup. Air\nflows above a radiator, and sunlight reaches Earth across space. All\nthree processes transfer energy, but by different mechanisms.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and physical model<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong>conduction<\/strong>, energy passes through a material or\nbetween materials in contact. The material as a whole does not move\nalong the energy-transfer path. Metals commonly conduct energy well.<\/p>\n<p>In <strong>convection<\/strong>, energy is carried along by a moving\n<strong>fluid<\/strong> – a liquid or gas. Warm air can rise if a density\ndifference produces buoyancy-driven circulation; fans and pumps can also\nforce fluid motion.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Thermal radiation<\/strong> is energy transfer by\nelectromagnetic radiation. It requires no material medium, which is why\nsolar radiation crosses space. Multiple mechanisms often act\nsimultaneously. These names describe energy-transfer routes, not three\ntypes of heat stored inside a body.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original draft diagram:<\/strong> Three independent panels\nshowing contact conduction, circulating matter and radiation waves.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_020_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three panels showing conduction through a solid, convection flow, and radiation across a vacuum.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three panels showing conduction through a\nsolid, convection flow, and radiation across a vacuum.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Conduction, convection and radiation transfer energy through\ndifferent mechanisms.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and application<\/h2>\n<p>A vacuum-insulated bottle reduces conduction and convection through\nits walls. A reflective coating can reduce radiative exchange. Its cap\nand structural connections may still conduct energy. <strong>Perfect\ninsulation does not exist<\/strong>, but different designs reduce losses\nby different amounts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe investigation<\/h2>\n<p>Put identical containers of <strong>lukewarm<\/strong>, not hot, water\non two safe supports such as wood and metal, and record their\ntemperatures at matched intervals. This is a qualitative comparison, not\nproof of a single mechanism: the vessel, surrounding air and contact\narea also matter. Do not use flames or hot metal.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can energy from the Sun cross the vacuum of space?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which mechanism requires bulk fluid motion?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the handle of a metal spoon often warm faster than that of\na wooden one?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Classify these: rising warm air by a window; energy crossing a solid\nwall.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Electromagnetic radiation travels through a vacuum.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Convection, which transports energy with moving liquid or gas.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Metals usually have greater thermal conductivity than wood.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Air motion: convection; through a solid wall: mainly\nconduction.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and transition<\/h2>\n<p>In a real energy balance, conduction, convection and radiation\noverlap. Thermal imaging indicates surface temperatures; it does not\ndirectly measure heat flow. Continue with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics curriculum: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n3.1, 3.4 and 3.11 · Thermal behaviour, heat transfer and energy · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/waermelehre/waermetransport/grundwissen/waermetransport\">LEIFIphysik\n– Heat transfer<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Conduction, convection, radiation and combinations\nof mechanisms · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/waermelehre/waermetransport/grundwissen/waermestrahlung-temperaturstrahlung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Thermal radiation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Electromagnetic energy transfer in a vacuum ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M4-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M4-L4 – Thermal expansion and water’s density anomaly<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Temperature, heat and particles<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Bridges and rails must allow for temperature changes. The volume of\nmany liquids also changes as they warm or cool. Near its freezing point,\nwater behaves unusually.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and physical model<\/h2>\n<p>Many solids <strong>expand on heating<\/strong>. For a thin rod over a\nmoderate temperature range, a linear approximation gives the change in\nlength:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta l=\\alpha\\,l_0\\,\\Delta T.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis initial length,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe temperature change and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe material’s linear expansion coefficient in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{K^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis approximation applies within a limited range. Engineered structures\nmust allow movement or accommodate resulting stresses.<\/p>\n<p>For <strong>liquid water<\/strong>, density reaches a maximum at about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunder ordinary pressure conditions. Cooling from around\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nslightly reduces the volume of a fixed mass, but cooling from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases it. Ice is less dense than liquid water and floats, allowing\nlakes to freeze at the surface.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original draft diagram:<\/strong> A lengthening rod and a\nqualitative water-density graph peaking near 4 °C.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_021_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two rods at different temperatures on the left and a qualitative water-density curve peaking near 4 °C on the right.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two rods at different temperatures on the\nleft and a qualitative water-density curve peaking near 4 °C on the\nright.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Heating can lengthen a rod, while liquid water has a density\nanomaly near 4 °C.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and application<\/h2>\n<p>Consider a steel rod of initial length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l_0=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor this calculation, <strong>assume<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=12\\cdot10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{K^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta T=50\\,\\mathrm{K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0012<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta l=(12\\cdot10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{K^{-1}})(2.0\\,\\mathrm{m})(50\\,\\mathrm{K})=0.0012\\,\\mathrm{m}=1.2\\,\\mathrm{mm}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The model assumes even heating and unrestrained expansion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe investigation<\/h2>\n<p>Do not try to measure expansion by heating metal yourself. Use\ndiagrams or a <strong>simulation<\/strong> of an expansion joint. For\nwater, sketch a qualitative density graph from provided temperatures.\nMeasuring small density changes accurately requires suitable laboratory\nequipment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l_0=1.5\\,\\mathrm{m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=12\\cdot10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{K^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta T=20\\,\\mathrm{K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At roughly what temperature is liquid water most dense?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why may rails require room for expansion?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does water become denser or less dense when warmed from 0 °C to 4\n°C?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.00036<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.36<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta l=12\\cdot10^{-6}\\cdot1.5\\cdot20\\,\\mathrm{m}=0.00036\\,\\mathrm{m}=0.36\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">About\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndepending slightly on conditions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Thermal expansion can create stress and deformation if movement is\nconstrained.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Denser: its density increases as it warms towards about 4 °C.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Linear expansion is an approximation. The plotted water-density\nrelation is <strong>schematic, not measured data<\/strong>; density also\ndepends slightly on pressure and composition. Continue with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics curriculum: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n3.1, 3.4 and 3.11 · Thermal behaviour, heat transfer and energy · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/waermelehre/ausdehnung-bei-erwaermung/aufgabe/anomalie-des-wassers\">LEIFIphysik\n– Density anomaly of water<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Density maximum of water near 4 °C and\nqualitative model · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M4-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M4-L5 – Specific heat capacity and temperature change<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Temperature, heat and particles<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A small cup of water can be warmed faster than a large one using the\nsame heater power. The resulting temperature rise for a given energy\ninput also depends on the material.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and physical model<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Specific heat capacity<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes the energy required per kilogram for a temperature increase of\none kelvin. For heating <strong>without a phase change<\/strong>, using\nan approximately constant value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>c<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=m\\,c\\,\\Delta T.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis energy transferred as heat,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis mass,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis specific heat capacity in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{J/(kg\\,K)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the temperature change in kelvin. If no other relevant energy flows\noccur,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequals the internal-energy change. For classroom calculations, water can\nbe assigned\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>4190<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c\\approx4190\\,\\mathrm{J/(kg\\,K)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA difference of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20\\,\\mathrm{K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original draft diagram:<\/strong> Two model lines of energy\nversus temperature rise for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof water, using the same value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nValues are explicitly model values, not measurements.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_022_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two model lines for 0.5 and 1.0 kg of water, reaching 41.9 and 83.8 kJ at 20 K.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two model lines for 0.5 and 1.0 kg of\nwater, reaching 41.9 and 83.8 kJ at 20 K.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>For the same material and temperature rise, heat transfer scales\nwith mass.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and application<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=0.50\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof water and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta T=20\\,\\mathrm{K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4190<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>41<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>900<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>41.9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=0.50\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\cdot4190\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{J}}{\\mathrm{kg\\,K}}\\cdot20\\,\\mathrm{K}=41\\,900\\,\\mathrm{J}=41.9\\,\\mathrm{kJ}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunder otherwise identical conditions, the value is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>83.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">83.8\\,\\mathrm{kJ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the simple model, twice the mass requires twice the energy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe investigation<\/h2>\n<p>Design a <strong>thought experiment<\/strong> comparing two similarly\ninsulated containers with different masses of water. Calculate expected\nenergy inputs from given data. A real electrical-heating investigation\nmust use approved school equipment and teacher supervision; input power\nand heat losses would also need measurement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.25\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof water heated by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm{K}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the SI unit of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a kettle’s electrical input not exactly equal the energy\nreceived by the water?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What assumption fails during melting?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4190<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>475<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>10.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=0.25\\cdot4190\\cdot10=10\\,475\\,\\mathrm{J}\\approx10.5\\,\\mathrm{kJ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{J/(kg\\,K)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Some energy heats the container and surroundings; further losses may\noccur.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A phase change occurs, so energy changes the state rather than only\nthe temperature.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The formula requires a suitable material value and works best over\nrestricted temperature ranges with no phase change. Plotted values are\npredictions rather than measured data. Continue with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics curriculum: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n3.1, 3.4 and 3.11 · Thermal behaviour, heat transfer and energy · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/waermelehre/innere-energie-waermekapazitaet/grundwissen/spezifische-waermekapazitaet\">LEIFIphysik\n– Specific heat capacity<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Equation Q = mcΔT, water value 4190\nJ/(kg·K) · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M4-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M4-L6 – Melting, vaporisation and latent heat<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Temperature, heat and particles<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Ice can melt near\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhile energy keeps entering. Why does its temperature not immediately\nrise? Under these conditions, the added energy changes the arrangement\nand interactions of particles.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and physical model<\/h2>\n<p>Pure substances often change phase at approximately fixed\ntemperatures while pressure remains constant, for example during\n<strong>melting<\/strong> and <strong>boiling<\/strong>. The transferred\nenergy is called <strong>latent heat<\/strong> or phase-change energy.\nFor a complete phase change of mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na useful approximation is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>L<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q_{\\mathrm{phase}}=m\\,L.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the specific latent heat in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{J/kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMelting and vaporisation require energy input; freezing and condensation\nrelease energy. Liquid can <strong>evaporate below boiling\ntemperature<\/strong>, which can cool the remaining liquid.<\/p>\n<p>An ideal heating curve for pure water at normal atmospheric pressure\nhas sloping parts and nearly flat sections around\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nActual results depend on conditions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original draft diagram:<\/strong> Schematic\ntemperature-versus-energy curve with melting and boiling plateaus,\nexplicitly not to scale.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_023_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Idealised curve with flat sections at 0 and 100 °C under ordinary atmospheric pressure.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Idealised curve with flat sections at 0\nand 100 °C under ordinary atmospheric pressure.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Energy can enter during a phase transition without raising the\ntemperature.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and application<\/h2>\n<p>Take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof ice <strong>already at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/strong>.\nUsing an approximate specific latent heat of fusion of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>335<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_f=335\\,\\mathrm{kJ/kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe energy needed to melt it is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>335<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>67.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=0.20\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\cdot335\\,\\mathrm{kJ/kg}=67.0\\,\\mathrm{kJ}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Energy to warm colder ice to 0 °C, or warm the resulting water above\n0 °C, is <strong>additional<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe investigation<\/h2>\n<p>Examine a <strong>provided<\/strong> temperature–time graph and mark\nintervals where temperature changes little. A real school investigation\ncan use small pieces of ice and <strong>lukewarm<\/strong> water with\nsupervision. Boiling water, steam and pressurised containers are not\nsuitable self-directed activities. Compare measurements with the\nidealised curve.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can temperature remain nearly constant during melting?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the energy needed to melt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.10\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof ice at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,^\\circ\\mathrm{C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>335<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_f=335\\,\\mathrm{kJ/kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the reverse of condensation?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=mc\\Delta T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalone not describe a phase-change plateau?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Energy is used mainly to change phase and particle arrangement.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>335<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>33.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=0.10\\cdot335=33.5\\,\\mathrm{kJ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Vaporisation: liquid to gas.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">On a plateau\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis almost zero even though substantial phase-change energy is\ntransferred.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Plateaus describe an ideal pure substance at nearly constant\npressure. Mixtures, heat losses and measurement lag modify actual\ncurves. Boiling point depends on pressure. Continue with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L7<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics curriculum: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n3.1, 3.4 and 3.11 · Thermal behaviour, heat transfer and energy · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/waermelehre/innere-energie-waermekapazitaet/grundwissen/spezifische-schmelz-und-verdampfungswaerme\">LEIFIphysik\n– Specific latent heat of fusion and vaporisation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Energy transfer\nin phase changes and the particle model · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/waermelehre/innere-energie-waermekapazitaet/aufgabe/zeit-temperatur-diagramm\">LEIFIphysik\n– Time–temperature graph<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Heating plateaus and approximate latent\nheat of fusion of ice 335 kJ/kg · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M4-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M4-L7 – Thermal insulation and an energy balance<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Temperature, heat and particles<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>In winter, a heated building can cool if energy escapes faster than\nthe heating system supplies it. Effective insulation reduces the\nrequired energy input; insulation does not generate heat itself.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and physical model<\/h2>\n<p>Energy is conserved for a <strong>clearly defined system<\/strong>. A\nsimplified balance is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U=E_{\\mathrm{in}}-E_{\\mathrm{out}},<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>provided the terms include the relevant forms of transfer for that\nmodel. Inputs include heating and sunlight; outputs include energy\ntransfer through walls, roof, windows and ventilation. The signs depend\non the system boundary.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Insulation<\/strong> reduces conduction and often convection.\nMultiple glazing can reduce heat transfer, while reflective surfaces can\nalter radiation. Gaps let air carry energy away. A <strong>thermal\nbridge<\/strong> is a path with relatively high heat flow, for example at\nsome junctions between building elements.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original draft diagram:<\/strong> Building cross-section with\nenergy-transfer paths through walls, roof and an air gap, plus a\nseparate input–output balance without invented measurements.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_024_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Building schematic with roof, wall and air pathways and an example balance of 600 kJ in versus 450 kJ out.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Building schematic with roof, wall and\nair pathways and an example balance of 600 kJ in versus 450 kJ\nout.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The internal-energy change is modelled as energy in minus energy\nout.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and application<\/h2>\n<p>In a chosen time interval, a model room receives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">500\\,\\mathrm{kJ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom heating and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{kJ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom sunlight. It loses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>450<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">450\\,\\mathrm{kJ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacross its boundary during that interval. For this defined model:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>450<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>150<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U=(500+100-450)\\,\\mathrm{kJ}=150\\,\\mathrm{kJ}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This does <strong>not directly imply<\/strong> a particular increase\nin room temperature: materials, masses, heat capacities and other\nprocesses must be included.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe investigation<\/h2>\n<p>Make a <strong>calculated comparison table<\/strong> for two model\nrooms receiving the same input but with different assumed losses.\nEvaluate sealing draughts, insulating surfaces and appropriate\nventilation. Altering real building fabric is not a classroom\nexperiment. Ventilation and moisture requirements must not be\nignored.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>800<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{in}}=800\\,\\mathrm{kJ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>620<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{out}}=620\\,\\mathrm{kJ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is insulation not an energy source?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name two different heat-loss pathways in a house.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can we not infer “the room warms by 5 °C” from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalone?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>800<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>620<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>180<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta U=800-620=180\\,\\mathrm{kJ}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It reduces transfer rates; it does not create energy.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Conduction through building materials and convection/ventilation\nlosses, or thermal radiation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The effective heat capacity, mass and temperature distribution and\nfurther conditions are unknown.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations and transition<\/h2>\n<p>A real building balance needs time dependence, air exchange,\nmoisture, radiation, thermal bridges and measurements. The diagram is a\nmodel, not an energy certificate. The next module starts with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L1 – Electric charge and\nelectrostatic forces<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics curriculum: topics and content<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n3.1, 3.4 and 3.11 · Thermal behaviour, heat transfer and energy · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/waermelehre/waermetransport/grundwissen/waermetransport\">LEIFIphysik\n– Heat transfer<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Conduction, convection, radiation and combinations\nof mechanisms · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
